## 2. Electrical — the survey that saves the project

### Neutral in switch boxes

The single most common technical obstacle in Ontario and Quebec retrofit work is the absence of a
neutral conductor in the switch box. **This must be found now, not on installation day.**

| Room | Switch | Neutral present? | Box depth adequate? | Multi-way? | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| | | ☐ Yes ☐ No | ☐ Yes ☐ No | ☐ Yes ☐ No | |
| | | ☐ Yes ☐ No | ☐ Yes ☐ No | ☐ Yes ☐ No | |
| | | ☐ Yes ☐ No | ☐ Yes ☐ No | ☐ Yes ☐ No | |

Solutions by case:

| Situation | Approach |
|---|---|
| Neutral present, standard box | Standard smart switch or dimmer, or micromodule behind the existing switch |
| No neutral, standard box | No-neutral capable dimmer designed for the North American market. **Verify LED fixture compatibility** |
| No neutral, no room in box | Module at the fixture canopy, or bulb-level control. Requires access to the fixture |
| Multi-way (3-way, 4-way) | Companion devices from the **same** family. **Never mix vendors across a multi-way group** |

### Aluminium branch wiring

☐ Present ☐ Not present ☐ Not determined

Common in certain 1960s and 1970s Canadian housing. If present, it requires pigtailing with approved
connectors by a licensed electrician. It is an insurance issue in its own right, and a legitimate
upsell — but it must be quoted, not discovered.

